choice("Continue", B3-Ch06b-Abysmal, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06b-Abysmal)


ID: B3-Ch06b-Abysmal
TEXT:

"Look at you, finally fighting like a warrior again," Gontrok pants, wiping sweat from his brow. "Good. I thought all those years hiding in dwarf skin turned you soft. Now I can tell your parents their pathetic son still has some spine left. Not that they care. Every time I mention you, they pretend you never existed."

"Shut your mouth and fight, Gontrok--"

"Was it worth it, Hadrik?!" Gontrok roars over him, venom dripping from every syllable. "Throwing away your homeland, your clan, your family—all over one insignificant dwarf village?"

"Dwarf," Arraka's voice hisses from the amulet. "If you lose your head over this again..."

"I have it," Hadrik says, his tone dead and flat, though the veins on his forearms throb.

"What were you trying to prove, crawling through dwarven lands in their shape?" Gontrok demands, ignoring the amulet. "Atonement? You thought laying flowers on graves made up for butchering women and children? Face it, Hadrik—you are a killer like the rest of us. The difference is I do not whine like a whipped dog. I can butcher civilians in a war and look at myself in the mirror without running away."

"Dwarf," Arraka snaps. "If you let him rattle you, I will rip out your spine and--"

"I said I have it!" Hadrik roars, lunging forward with blinding fury.

Gontrok braces for the impact, but Hadrik snaps down to leprechaun size on the charge, slides between Gontrok's legs, and erupts back to human height behind him. He clamps a hand to the back of Gontrok's skull, drives his face violently into the cobblestones, and cocks his right fist to deliver a skull-shattering hammer blow.

His fist slams into a shimmering, semi-transparent barrier an inch above Gontrok's head. The force jars Hadrik's wrist. Gontrok twists free in the confusion, rolling backward to his feet, his gaze locking onto a narrow alleyway to our right.

"Your help was unnecessary, Captain," Gontrok snarls.

"Was it?" The captain's voice echoes from the shadows as he steps into the light. "Did not look under control from where I stood, recruit."

"You were watching the whole time?" Gontrok asks.

"Watching enough to know you are out of your depth," the captain says coldly. "I let you indulge your duel because you throw tantrums when interrupted, but that performance was abysmal. Step aside, Gontrok. We handle this now."

Gontrok trembles with suppressed fury as royal guards emerge from side streets and rooftops, closing the circle around us.

choice(""Hey, Gontrok, are you going to just let him insult you like that?"", B3-Ch06b-Insult, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06b-Insult)
choice(""Captain, you just interrupted a sacred duel! Do you have no honor?"", B3-Ch06b-Sacred, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06b-Sacred)
choice(""Yeah, get out of the way, Gontrok! Only real warriors are allowed here!"", B3-Ch06b-Warriors, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06b-Warriors)


ID: B3-Ch06b-Insult
TEXT:

"Hey, Gontrok, are you going to just let him insult you like that?"

"Shut up!" Gontrok snaps, glaring back at me. "I do not need your advice. The captain can jump off a cliff. I am not stepping down from this fight."

"What you want is irrelevant," the captain says. "They will be dead before you lay another finger on them. Observe."

The words barely leave his lips before crushing pressure slams down on us. The sheer gravitational force drives my knees into the stone, pinning our limbs in an instant. Groaning under the weight, I strain to tilt my chin upward.

High above, the sky is choked with massive stone boulders suspended in midair, hovering like executioners' axes.

The earth elementalist drops the first volley, hurling several boulders straight at our pinned formation. Before the rocks crush us, Daren wrenches his sword arm upward and cuts a sharp horizontal line through the air. The captain's aura grip unravels instantly.

Kate reacts on instinct, throwing a canopy of reinforced ice over our heads. Hadrik launches into the air with a deafening war cry, pulverizing the heaviest falling boulder with a midair punch. Stone shrapnel gouges through Kate's ice canopy, but we scramble clear of the falling rubble.

"Hah!" Gontrok barks a harsh laugh. "What happened, Captain? Thought you had everything handled. Need a hand?"

"Quiet, fool," the captain barks. "They would be dead if you followed the plan."

"Shove your plan," Gontrok snarls. "I am nobody's lapdog—especially not yours."

"Illuna," I whisper, keeping my eyes forward. "If I keep them arguing, can you bring out those giant flying water serpents from yesterday?"

"I have been weaving the construct since they stepped into the street," Illuna murmurs. "I can release them, but these mages will shred them in open air. They need to be pinned first."

"I can drop a freeze wave across the perimeter," Kate whispers. "Huddle close so I do not catch you by mistake."

"Hadrik," I hiss under my breath. "Hadrik, hear me?... Damn it, he is not looking."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06b-Crumble, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06b-Crumble)


ID: B3-Ch06b-Sacred
TEXT:

"Captain, you just interrupted a sacred duel! Do you have no honor?"

"There is no honor in executing murderers," the captain says coldly. "You butchered the earl, and you deserve nothing. Enough talk. Die."

The words barely leave his lips before crushing pressure slams down on us. The sheer gravitational force drives my knees into the stone, pinning our limbs in an instant. Groaning under the weight, I strain to tilt my chin upward.

High above, the sky is choked with massive stone boulders suspended in midair, hovering like executioners' axes.

The earth elementalist drops the first volley, hurling several boulders straight at our pinned formation. Before the rocks crush us, Daren wrenches his sword arm upward and cuts a sharp horizontal line through the air. The captain's aura grip unravels instantly.

Kate reacts on instinct, throwing a canopy of reinforced ice over our heads. Hadrik launches into the air with a deafening war cry, pulverizing the heaviest falling boulder with a midair punch. Stone shrapnel gouges through Kate's ice canopy, but we scramble clear of the falling rubble.

"Hah!" Gontrok barks a harsh laugh. "What happened, Captain? Thought you had everything handled. Need a hand?"

"Quiet, fool," the captain barks. "They would be dead if you followed the plan."

"Shove your plan," Gontrok snarls. "I am nobody's lapdog—especially not yours."

"Illuna," I whisper, keeping my eyes forward. "If I keep them arguing, can you bring out those giant flying water serpents from yesterday?"

"I have been weaving the construct since they stepped into the street," Illuna murmurs. "I can release them, but these mages will shred them in open air. They need to be pinned first."

"I can drop a freeze wave across the perimeter," Kate whispers. "Huddle close so I do not catch you by mistake."

"Hadrik," I hiss under my breath. "Hadrik, hear me?... Damn it, he is not looking."

